// divzero_own_panic.tk -- D82 amended (the PR's review): the guard calls the
// RUNTIME's `panic`, and checks that it is that one. `decl_find("panic")`
// alone only proves some declaration of the name exists, so a program
// carrying its own `panic` and no `#include "rt.tk"` used to compile into a
// guard that called the no-op and let the division run on (measured: exit
// 99, no abort). The declaration's own file has to be `lib/rt.tk`
// (`tk_div_ends`, teko_ternary.tk), so this program is refused exactly like
// one with no `panic` at all (`tests/refuse/divzero_no_rt.tk`).
//
// The other half is mc's, measured and left there: a program that declares
// its own `panic` AND includes the runtime is `function declared twice`,
// ahead of this pass.
//
// expect-refuse: teko: an integer division needs #include rt.tk
// expect-refuse-line: 22

i64 panic(str msg) { return 0; }

i64 main() {
    i64 n = 0;
    i64 a = 10;
    i64 q = a / n;
    return 99;
}
